include "../../linux.mereo"

-- `NAME is VALUE` opens a name if nothing has and assigns it if something has,
-- so a scope reaching for a fresh temp silently takes one a sibling used -- and
-- reads what the sibling left. Here `two` means its own `v` and gets `one`'s 7,
-- so the program printed 14 rather than 107, in silence.
--
-- C++ gives each block its own `v` and warns that the second is used
-- uninitialised. mereo cannot say that, because the read is not uninitialised:
-- every scalar has a value, it is simply the previous scope's. What it can see
-- is the shape -- this scope WRITES the name later, so it meant one of its own.

program goes
  w is 8 bytes
  t is 0
  one goes
    v is 7
    t is t + v
  end
  two goes
    t is t + v
    v is 100
  end
  [w + 0 : 8] is t
end
